## Space for three

Twenty-two square metres under the roofline, with a double bed and a separate single arranged so that nobody has to climb over anybody to reach their own. The ceiling follows the pitch of the roof on one side and a long skylight runs along it, which is what gives this room its character: on a clear night you get the sky, and in the morning the light arrives from above rather than through the curtains.

The walls are papered in a pale brick pattern that keeps the room feeling open despite the slope, and the floor is wood rather than the carpet found lower down. A tall timber-clad unit holds the wardrobe and frames the head of the double bed. A black metal console carries the kettle, the coffee machine and cups, with the flat-screen TV mounted on the wall opposite.

The bathroom has a corner shower, a wall-hung basin and a heated towel rail.

It is a useful room for three friends travelling together, or a family with one older child who would rather not share a bed. Bear in mind that it sits at the top of the house.

Free WiFi, a seating area, and tea and coffee throughout your stay. Reception is staffed around the clock, and there is luggage storage downstairs if you arrive before three or leave on a late train.
